.TH ASL 1 "2026-10-12" "ASL 1.42.313" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
asl \- macro cross assembler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B asl
.RI [ options ] " source" " [source ...]"
.br
.B asl \-help
.br
.B asl \-version
.br
.B asl \-cpu list
.SH DESCRIPTION
The flamewing fork of Macro Assembler AS assembles sources for multiple target
processors. Select a CPU in the source or with \-cpu. Source names without an
extension receive .asm; default code output uses .p. Convert .p code files
with p2bin or p2hex when a raw binary or hexadecimal file is required.
.PP
Windows executables have an .exe suffix. MSYS2 also provides asw.
The full user manual documents source syntax and target-specific details.
.SH INVOCATION
Options can appear before or after sources and apply to every source in the
invocation. Word options are case-insensitive; single-letter options are
case-sensitive. A minus sign enables an option and a plus sign negates or
resets it. Single-letter options can be combined, for example \-nx.
Slash-prefixed options are supported by Windows builds; Unix builds treat
absolute slash-prefixed paths as filenames. GNU-style \-\-help and the
\-\- end-of-options marker are not supported.
.PP
Quote wildcard patterns to let the assembler expand them, and quote paths
containing spaces. Prefix filenames beginning with an option marker with a
directory such as ./ (or .\\ on Windows).
.PP
Optional arguments can consume a following source name. Use \-g map source.asm
or source.asm \-g rather than \-g source.asm. Options requiring arguments
receive them as a separate command-line argument.
