.SH OPTION DETAILS
\-A balances remaining macro/structure trees; symbols use hash tables.
\-x adds diagnostic context, and \-xx adds the source line.
\-q suppresses progress; repeating it also suppresses source MESSAGE output.
\-warnranges changes eligible evaluator range errors into warnings, but does
not soften every directive-specific hard range check.
.PP
Repeat \-i for multiple include directories. A path list uses : on Unix and ;
on Windows. \-t mask enables listing bits; +t mask disables them (0..511,
default 511). \-maxerrors 0 means no error-count limit. The defaults for
\-maxinclevel and \-maxsympass are 200 and 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ASCMD
Preset options, processed before command-line options; may refer to a key
file with @filename. Preset options and key files can also supply source names.
.TP
.B AS_MSGPATH
One external-catalog directory. Catalogs are embedded by default; configure
ASL_EMBED_MESSAGES=OFF to use external .msg files. The reader searches the
current directory, explicit executable directory, AS_MSGPATH (or PATH when
unset), then the compiled-in library directory.
.TP
.B LC_MESSAGES, LC_ALL, LANG
Catalog language overrides, checked in this order. German and English are
supported; English is the default. Country information can also select the
language. Node-based WebAssembly builds inherit the host environment.
.TP
.B USEANSI
Set to N to disable ANSI console control sequences.
.SH KEY FILES
Use @filename on the command line to insert options at that position.
Options and their arguments must be on the same line; lines beginning with
; are comments. Key files cannot reference other key files. The line buffer
is 1024 bytes including the terminator. Key-file and ASCMD parsing split on
whitespace without shell quoting; use command-line arguments for paths with
spaces. A leading + can undo an earlier preset option, for example +L.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful assembly, help, version or CPU-list request; warnings may occur.
.TP
.B 1
No input sources supplied. No interactive filename prompt is issued.
.TP
.B 2
Assembly errors in one or more sources.
.TP
.B 3
Fatal assembly or I/O failure.
.TP
.B 4
Invalid command-line/preset option or key-file startup failure.
.TP
.B 255
Catalog/resource or internal initialization failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
asl \-cpu 68000 \-i include \-o output.p source.asm
asl \-L \-olist source.lst \-g map source.asm
asl \-nx '*.asm'
asl @build-options.key source.asm
asl \-help
asl \-cpu list
.fi
